+**Whole numbers are coarser than the settings.** The three settings are the
+altitude in thousands of feet, the Mach number (speed as a fraction of the
+speed of sound) and the throttle angle in degrees; the
+[C-MAPSS resources page](../../../../docs/resources/cmapss.md) shows how that
+reading was established. Rounding the Mach number to a whole number merges
+conditions that differ in it. On FD002 and FD004, the two sub-datasets with six
+conditions, all four files (training and test of each) give the same six
+combinations, measured on 2026-10-02:
+
+| Altitude, rounded | Mach number, to two decimals | Throttle angle | Mach number, rounded |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 0 | 0.00 | 100 | 0 |
+| 10 | 0.25 | 100 | 0 |
+| 20 | 0.70 | 100 | 1 |
+| 25 | 0.62 | 60 | 1 |
+| 35 | 0.84 | 100 | 1 |
+| 42 | 0.84 | 100 | 1 |
+
+Rounded, the Mach number keeps only 0 and 1, and the throttle angle has only
+two values, so neither tells the six conditions apart. The count still comes
+out at six because altitude alone does: its six rounded values all differ. A
+dataset with two conditions at the same altitude, differing only in Mach
+number, would be counted as one. The merge touches only the count: k-means
+then places its centres using the unrounded settings.
+
